diff --git a/docs/hdr.md b/docs/hdr.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..8db00a1
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/hdr.md
@@ -0,0 +1,127 @@
+# HDR output
+
+`hdr=1` in `bbport.ini` (`BB_HDR=1`, the menu's "HDR output", applied on restart) presents the
+game in an HDR swapchain when the display offers one (Windows HDR on): HDR10 (BT.2020, PQ,
+`A2B10G10R10`) or scRGB (linear BT.709 in FP16). The game itself only makes an 8-bit SDR frame;
+`hdr_output.frag` encodes it. SDR white is shown at `hdr_paper_white` nits (80-500, default 200),
+nothing goes above `hdr_peak` (400-10000, default 1000). The settings menu is drawn into its own
+layer and composed at paper white. SDR is decoded with a 2.2 power curve (the piecewise sRGB curve
+lifts Bloodborne's shadows into grey).
+
+How the highlights get above paper white is `hdr_mode` (`BB_HDR_MODE`, the menu's "HDR mode",
+live):
+
+| mode | highlights from | midtones | UI |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| `native` (default) | the game's own linear HDR scene, before its tonemap | exactly the SDR picture | paper white |
+| `expand` | the clipped SDR picture (a guess) | exactly the SDR picture | expanded like the rest |
+
+## Expand
+
+Above a knee (linear 0.8) the brightest channel of the SDR frame is stretched so that SDR white
+reaches `1 + (peak / paper - 1) * hdr_boost` times paper white (boost 0..1, default 0.25: 400
+nits with the defaults). Hue and saturation stay. Anything near SDR white is expanded, a lamp and
+a white menu text alike.
+
+## Native
+
+The game's tonemap compresses its HDR scene into SDR: midtones roughly by an exposure, highlights
+by a shoulder that ends at SDR white. Native mode keeps the game's SDR frame (its grading,
+contrast, saturation, vignette, fog colour, bloom and UI) and multiplies its highlights by how
+much brighter the scene really is there, measured every frame on the GPU:
+
+1. **Scene luma** (`hdr_native.comp` LUMA): before the post-processing combine (compute shader
+   `9a9cf8a9`, the upscaler's hook; after FSR/DLSS/TAA wrote its output into the scene color,
+   so output-sized at 1080p output). The RGBA16F scene color's brightest channel at half size:
+   the 2x2 maximum (highlights) and the brightest channel of the 2x2 average (exposure). A
+   reduced scene (scaled presets) is resolved into the guest image first, as the game's own
+   compute passes reading it do.
+2. **UI-free copy and exposure** (SNAPSHOT, EXPOSURE): at the first UI pass (at 1080p output
+   the first Scaleform draw: vertex shader `34e8a281` or another of
+   `UiComposition::MovieShader`, into the game's RGBA8 UI target; with scaled presets the
+   output-size UI image right after the upscaler or background copy wrote it) the tonemapped
+   frame is copied bit for bit, and
+   `log2(SDR / scene)` is averaged over the SDR midtones (linear brightest channel 0.02-0.45, at
+   least 1/200 of the pixels): what the game's curve does to the scene there. Bloom lights up
+   SDR midtones around lamps where the scene is dark; samples far from the last exposure count
+   less (Gaussian weight, one stop), so a uniform change still goes through. Smoothed over
+   frames (10% a frame); after frames without a scene it starts over.
+3. **Gain map** (GAIN): at the display pass that copies the finished frame (UI included) into a
+   display buffer, the first pass of the next frame. Per pixel of the finished frame:
+   - UI: where the frame differs from the UI-free copy (HUD, menus, subtitles, their translucent
+     backgrounds), gain 1;
+   - below the knee (linear brightest channel 0.5) gain 1: the SDR picture exactly; above it the
+     gain fades in (smoothstep to SDR white);
+   - the scene's brightness on the SDR scale is `exposure * scene`, with the brightest scene
+     pixel of the 2x2 luma texels around (4x4 scene pixels: the footprint does not shimmer with
+     the upscalers' sub-pixel jitter). Where the SDR frame is brighter than that (bloom, glare:
+     they are added after the scene color) the gain stays 1; else the SDR pixel goes towards it:
+     `gain = (scene / sdr) ^ (sqrt(boost) * fade)`;
+   - the result goes smoothly into the peak (unchanged up to halfway between SDR white and the
+     peak, then towards the peak with the same slope), so very bright sources keep some
+     gradation.
+
+   The map (R16F, the finished frame's size, negative where UI was found) belongs to that
+   display buffer. The flip of a display buffer comes a frame later in the command stream; the
+   presenter takes the buffer's map when it prepares the flip (`Presenter::PrepareFrame`,
+   exchanged with the frame's previous map, no copy), so a frame and its map always match.
+4. **Encode** (`hdr_output.frag`): the letterboxed SDR frame times the map (filtered, mapped
+   over the frame's rectangle in the window), capped at the peak.
+
+A frame without a scene (title, menus, loading screens, pre-rendered movies), or whose display
+pass reads another image than the UI-free copy came from (or whose UI target was not found),
+has no map: it is shown as plain SDR at paper white. A frame with a scene but no UI pass (in-engine
+cutscenes without subtitles) is its own UI-free copy.
+
+`hdr_boost` in native mode is the share of the scene's highlight range shown, in log2:
+`sqrt(boost)`. The default 0.25 shows half of it (a lamp the scene has 10x brighter than SDR
+white comes out about 3.2x), 1 all of it (up to the peak), 0 none (the SDR picture).
+
+HDR metadata (`VK_EXT_hdr_metadata`): max mastering luminance is the peak, MaxFALL paper white;
+MaxCLL is the peak in native mode and expand's SDR white level in expand mode. It changes with
+the settings, not per frame.
+
+### Cost
+
+Per frame, a handful of commands recorded on the recording threads (`Scheduler::Record`), none
+per draw, no readback, no wait: one dispatch at each of the three points (and a one-group sum),
+the encode samples one more image. GPU: the scene read once (RGBA16F), the frame read twice and
+copied once (RGBA8), a half-size RG16F and a full-size R16F written, about 55 MB at 1080p.
+Measured (`hdr-native-shader-test --bench <w> <h>`, all passes with their barriers, RTX 5060):
+0.18 ms at 1920x1080, 0.37 ms at 2560x1440, 0.79 ms at 3840x2160 (in the game the scene luma
+stays at the guest's 1920x1080 for larger outputs, so a little less). Native mode off (or HDR
+off): nothing.
+
+### Debug view
+
+`BB_HDR_DEBUG=1` shows the gain instead of the picture: the frame in grey at a third of paper
+white, the gain over it from blue (a little) through green and yellow to red (the peak), UI that
+native mode keeps at paper white in magenta. The square in the top left corner is green while
+native mode has the frame's gain map and orange while the frame has none and is shown as SDR (in
+expand mode the heat map shows expand's gain).
+
+The log says which mode runs (`HDR output: HDR10 (PQ, BT.2020), mode native (...)`, again when
+it is changed in the menu) and, in native mode, when frames stop or start having a gain map
+(`HDR native: ...`, at most every 5 s, with the number of switches in between).
+
+### Tests
+
+`ninja -C out/gpu hdr-native-shader-test` runs the passes on a synthetic frame (plain Vulkan,
+any 1.3 device; `BB_TEST_GPU=<index>` picks one): the luma's 2x2 max/average with NaN and inf
+texels, the bit-exact copy, the exposure of a known tonemap (exact to 0.03 stops) and its
+resistance to bloom (0.27 stops off on the first frame, 0.001 after 60), empty frames, and the
+gain map: exactly 1 in the midtones and on UI, the scene's ratio to the power of the strength
+into the peak on an 8x highlight, 1 on bloom brighter than the scene. `settings-test` covers
+`hdr_mode`.
+
+### Limits
+
+- The scene color is the pre-post scene: bloom, depth of field and motion blur are only in the
+  SDR frame. Bloom halos stay at SDR brightness; a blurred highlight gets the gain where its
+  sharp source is (the 4x4 footprint softens that).
+- One exposure for the whole frame: the vignette darkens the SDR corners but not the scene, so
+  bright highlights in the corners lose a little of the vignette.
+- Scaled presets (output other than 1080p): the scene luma is at the guest resolution, jittered
+  when an upscaler runs; the map is interpolated to the output.
+- `BB_UPSCALER=none` disables the frame analysis the passes rely on: native mode shows every frame
+  as SDR (use expand mode instead).
diff --git a/docs/pipeline_compiles.md b/docs/pipeline_compiles.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..dfb8088
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/pipeline_compiles.md
@@ -0,0 +1,115 @@
+# Pipeline compiles and the waits behind them
+
+A pipeline first seen during play is compiled on the compile threads (`BB_ASYNC_PIPELINES`,
+default on). With a cold NVIDIA cache a compile takes 100-300 ms; entering an area brings
+20-90 at once. Pipelines known from the shader database are created at start (preload) and
+come out of the driver cache (`<serial>.vkcache`) quickly.
+
+## What a draw does with a pipeline still compiling
+
+Before (0.9.10 beta 1) the draw recording thread (stage B of the draw pipe) waited for it when
+it recorded the bind: every new pipeline stalled the frame by its compile time (user logs, RTX
+5060: `2 compiles 594 ms` -> a 607 ms frame, `18 compiles 1110 ms` -> 1665 ms), and the GPU
+command thread and the guest threads that waited for stage B (drains, see below) stalled with
+it. Now (`Rasterizer::DrawRecord`, `HandleIfReady`):
+
+- **Scene geometry is left out** while its pipeline compiles (`SkipUncompiledDraw`): draws with
+  the scene depth (the G-buffer pass's, which was also the scene depth the frame before) that
+  are not full-screen passes (more than 6 indices or instanced) - the draws the upscaler
+  jitters. G-buffer, light volumes, forward/transparent draws and effects drawn with the scene
+  depth. The frame draws these targets again from a clear, so the object is missing for the
+  frames the compile takes (pop-in), nothing more. Everything else of the draw runs as before
+  (targets, barriers, render pass, resources): only `vkCmdDraw` is not recorded. Never left
+  out: draws the game measures (occlusion queries: zero samples would hide their predicated
+  draws), indirect draws, compute dispatches, shadow maps, half-resolution effects,
+  post-processing and UI (what they draw may be kept over frames or read as a whole), and
+  draws during a frame capture. A pipeline's draws are left out for at most
+  `BB_SKIP_UNCOMPILED_MS` (1000) from its first left-out draw; then they wait for it. "A few
+  frames" at 120 FPS is 25-40 ms, below every compile measured, so the bound is in time.
+  `BB_SKIP_UNCOMPILED=0`: never left out.
+- **Everything else binds the pipeline on a recording thread** (`BindWhenCompiled`): the bind
+  becomes a command of its own, and the recording thread that records it into the command
+  buffer waits for the compile there. Stage B goes on with the next draws, so drains of the
+  GPU command thread (and the guest threads behind them) do not wait for the compile. The
+  frame itself still waits for it (its submission needs the command). `BB_LATE_PIPELINE_BIND=0`
+  (or commands recorded on the calling thread: `BB_VK_RECORD_THREAD=0`, direct segments):
+  stage B waits, as before.
+- **A thread that must wait compiles the pipeline itself** when no compile thread has started
+  it (`Pipeline::WaitReady`, `TakePipelineJob`): in a burst it was queued behind the other
+  pipelines first. `BB_PIPELINE_TAKE=0`: waits for the compile threads.
+
+An already compiled pipeline costs what it did: `HandleIfReady` is the load and branch
+`Handle()` was. The draw command gained a branch on the recording thread (bind or not).
+
+## Why not VK_EXT_graphics_pipeline_library
+
+DXVK links pipelines from libraries compiled when the game creates its shaders, long before the
+draws. Here (measured on the user's shader database, 855 graphics pipelines):
+
+- Shaders reach the port at the draw that needs them: the guest binary is translated there.
+  Libraries would be compiled at that draw too; only linking is fast.
+- All stages of a pipeline share descriptor set 0 (one push descriptor set; the vertex stage's
+  bindings follow the fragment stage's, and the vertex module is specialized on that offset).
+  Libraries need identically defined layouts, so they are shared only between pipelines with
+  the same stage modules: 780 distinct stage sets among the 855 pipelines (75, 9%, would reuse
+  libraries). Separate sets per stage would end push descriptors for one of them: a descriptor
+  set write per draw on the thread that limits the frame rate.
+- NVIDIA compiles each library stage about as fully as in a pipeline: a draw would wait for
+  the slower of the vertex and fragment libraries instead of both (perhaps 30-40% less), at
+  about twice the compile work (libraries and the optimized pipeline after them).
+
+The start line reports whether the device offers it (`graphics pipeline library offered (fast
+linking)`) for later work, e.g. if shader translation could run ahead of the draws.
+
+## Waits behind the draw recording thread (write faults, drains)
+
+`Stall: 335.8 ms frame; ... write faults 324.6 ms; GPU thread on CPU 328.1 ms (kernel 203.1
+ms)`, and from a boss fight `image create 575 ms`, `image refresh 608.6 ms` with `write faults
+605.1 ms`. Two different waits:
+
+- **Guest threads** handle their write faults with locks (`OnWriteFault`, `assume_locks`
+  false); they never drain. `TextureCache::InvalidateMemory` takes the texture cache's lock,
+  which stage B holds through `FindImage` (image creation, page protection) and `UpdateImage`
+  (`RefreshImage`: the upload): 0.5-0.6 s with memory short. Most faults are on buffer pages
+  (the game rewriting constants and vertices) that no image watches. The texture cache now
+  counts, per 4 KiB page, the tracked images that protect it (`WatchedPages`, kept with the
+  page manager's watchers, readable without the lock); a fault on a page no image watches
+  updates the buffer tracking only. Images learn of CPU writes through their own page
+  protection alone, so this is what the lock gave them: an untracked image is already dirty,
+  or new (dirty), or hash-checked (MaybeCpuDirty). `BB_FAULT_SKIP_TEXTURES=0`: the lock for
+  every fault. A fault on a page an image watches still waits for the lock (unchanged), and
+  a guest thread's fault on GPU-written data still needs the GPU thread for the readback.
+- **The GPU command thread** (stage A) drains before it handles its own faults (stream order:
+  the queued draws read the old bytes first; a readback records commands) and at many other
+  points. It waited with `SwitchToThread` in a loop for as long as stage B took: a core burnt
+  in the kernel while stage B (or a compile thread) needed it. Its waits (`Drain`,
+  `WaitForPacket`, a full ring) now spin, yield for `BB_DRAIN_SPIN_US` (500), then sleep until
+  stage B has run the packet they need (stage B checks after each packet whether stage A
+  sleeps for it: a load and a branch). `BB_DRAIN_SLEEP=0`: spin and yield as before. The wait
+  itself is as long as stage B's work: with compiles off stage B that is now mostly image
+  creation and uploads (memory pressure).
+
+## Statistics and log lines
+
+At start:
+
+    Pipelines: new ones compiled on 4 threads; draws of one still compiling: scene geometry left out for up to 1000 ms, other draws bind it on the recording threads (they wait for it) (BB_SKIP_UNCOMPILED, BB_LATE_PIPELINE_BIND); graphics pipeline library offered (fast linking), not used
+    Waits: GPU command thread drains sleep after a spin (stage B wakes them); write faults on pages no image watches leave the texture cache alone (BB_DRAIN_SLEEP, BB_FAULT_SKIP_TEXTURES)
+
+`BB_FRAME_STATS`, every 5 s when anything happened:
+
+    Pipelines: 18 compiled in the background (2 by the thread that needed them); 340 draws of 12 pipelines left out while compiling; 6 draws/dispatches bound on the recording threads; 3 waits, 210.4 ms, longest 120.3 ms
+
+and `(N/s slept)` in the `Draw pipe:` line. The `shader/pipeline compiles, ms` figure of
+`Frame stats` still adds the waits (now on recording threads too) to the translation time.
+
+## Risks
+
+- Left-out scene draws: an object (or light volume, decal, particle) appears a few frames
+  late the first time its pipeline is needed. A transparent layer whose pipeline is ready
+  over an opaque one still compiling shows over the background for those frames.
+- A recording thread waiting for a compile delays its segment, the submission and the guest's
+  fences after it, as stage B's wait did; ordered tasks (guest copies, fence signals) queue
+  behind it.
+- `ReplaceShader` (shader patching from the debug UI) destroys pipelines at once; a late bind
+  still queued for one would use it after that.
diff --git a/gpu/shadps4/video_core/host_shaders/hdr_native.comp b/gpu/shadps4/video_core/host_shaders/hdr_native.comp
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..048efd8
--- /dev/null
+++ b/gpu/shadps4/video_core/host_shaders/hdr_native.comp
@@ -0,0 +1,254 @@
+// SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0-or-later
+// bbport: native HDR output (hdr_mode=native, docs/hdr.md). The game tonemaps its linear HDR
+// scene into an 8-bit SDR frame. These passes measure how much brighter than that frame its own
+// scene is where the game's curve compressed the highlights, as a gain map the HDR encode
+// (hdr_output.frag) multiplies the finished SDR frame (UI included) with:
+//   LUMA      before the post-processing combine: the scene color's brightest channel at half
+//             size, the 2x2 maximum (highlights) and that of the 2x2 average (exposure).
+//   STATS     the exposure statistics: log2(SDR / scene) over the SDR midtones (bloom's outliers
+//             weighted down), one sum per group; with SNAPSHOT also a copy of the tonemapped
+//             frame before the first UI pass.
+//   EXPOSURE  one group: the sums added up, the exposure smoothed over frames.
+//   GAIN      at the display pass: the gain per pixel of the finished frame.
+// The midtones keep the SDR picture exactly (gain 1 below the knee); the game's look (grading,
+// contrast, vignette, fog colour) stays, only the highlights get brighter.
+
+#version 450
+#extension GL_EXT_samplerless_texture_functions : require
+
+float Max3(vec3 c) {
+    return max(c.r, max(c.g, c.b));
+}
+
+// As hdr_output.frag: the game's picture is made for SDR displays, which show it with 2.2.
+vec3 SdrToLinear(vec3 c) {
+    return pow(max(c, vec3(0.0)), vec3(2.2));
+}
+
+// What the game's curve does to the midtones, as log2(SDR linear / scene).
+struct Exposure {
+    float log_scale; // smoothed over frames
+    float valid;     // 1 once measured
+    float samples;   // midtone samples of the last measurement (debugging)
+    float frames;    // measurements so far
+};
+
+#if defined(LUMA)
+
+layout(local_size_x = 8, local_size_y = 8) in;
+
+layout(binding = 0) uniform texture2D scene_tex;
+layout(binding = 1, rg16f) uniform writeonly image2D luma_img;
+
+void main() {
+    const ivec2 texel = ivec2(gl_GlobalInvocationID.xy);
+    if (any(greaterThanEqual(texel, imageSize(luma_img)))) {
+        return;
+    }
+    const ivec2 last = textureSize(scene_tex, 0) - 1;
+    float peak = 0.0;
+    vec3 sum = vec3(0.0);
+    for (int y = 0; y < 2; ++y) {
+        for (int x = 0; x < 2; ++x) {
+            vec3 c = texelFetch(scene_tex, min(texel * 2 + ivec2(x, y), last), 0).rgb;
+            // A NaN from an effect must not spread; an overflow (inf) is just very bright.
+            c = clamp(mix(c, vec3(0.0), isnan(c)), vec3(0.0), vec3(65504.0));
+            peak = max(peak, Max3(c));
+            sum += c;
+        }
+    }
+    imageStore(luma_img, texel, vec4(peak, Max3(sum * 0.25), 0.0, 0.0));
+}
+
+#elif defined(STATS)
+
+layout(local_size_x = 16, local_size_y = 16) in;
+
+layout(binding = 0) uniform texture2D frame_tex; // UNORM view: the display-encoded values
+layout(binding = 1) uniform texture2D luma_tex;
+layout(std430, binding = 2) writeonly buffer Partials {
+    vec4 partials[]; // per group: weighted sum of log2(SDR / scene), weights, samples
+};
+layout(std430, binding = 3) readonly buffer State {
+    Exposure exposure;
+};
+#ifdef SNAPSHOT
+layout(binding = 4, rgba8) uniform writeonly image2D snapshot_img;
+#endif
+layout(push_constant) uniform Params {
+    uint reset; // the last exposure is stale (no measurement last frame)
+};
+
+// SDR midtones (linear brightest channel): above the toe of the game's curve, below its
+// shoulder, where it is close to a plain exposure.
+const float MID_LOW = 0.02;
+const float MID_HIGH = 0.45;
+// Bloom and glare light up SDR midtones around lamps where the scene is dark: samples far from
+// the last exposure (in stops) count less. A uniform shift (a new area, the eye adapting) moves
+// all samples alike and the weighted mean follows it.
+const float SPREAD = 1.0;
+
+shared vec3 group_sums[256];
+
+void main() {
+    const ivec2 size = textureSize(frame_tex, 0);
+    const ivec2 pixel = ivec2(gl_GlobalInvocationID.xy);
+    vec3 sample_sum = vec3(0.0);
+    if (all(lessThan(pixel, size))) {
+        const vec4 c = texelFetch(frame_tex, pixel, 0);
+#ifdef SNAPSHOT
+        // Bit for bit (8-bit values in and out): the display pass finds the UI as any change.
+        imageStore(snapshot_img, pixel, c);
+#endif
+        const float sdr = Max3(SdrToLinear(c.rgb));
+        const ivec2 luma_size = textureSize(luma_tex, 0);
+        const ivec2 at = min(ivec2((vec2(pixel) + 0.5) * vec2(luma_size) / vec2(size)),
+                             luma_size - 1);
+        const float scene = texelFetch(luma_tex, at, 0).g;
+        if (sdr >= MID_LOW && sdr <= MID_HIGH && scene > 1e-5) {
+            const float stops = clamp(log2(sdr / scene), -32.0, 32.0);
+            const float off = reset == 0u && exposure.valid > 0.0
+                                  ? (stops - exposure.log_scale) / SPREAD
+                                  : 0.0;
+            const float weight = exp(-0.5 * off * off) + 1e-6;
+            sample_sum = vec3(stops * weight, weight, 1.0);
+        }
+    }
+    const uint index = gl_LocalInvocationIndex;
+    group_sums[index] = sample_sum;
+    barrier();
+    for (uint stride = 128u; stride > 0u; stride >>= 1u) {
+        if (index < stride) {
+            group_sums[index] += group_sums[index + stride];
+        }
+        barrier();
+    }
+    if (index == 0u) {
+        partials[gl_WorkGroupID.y * gl_NumWorkGroups.x + gl_WorkGroupID.x] =
+            vec4(group_sums[0], 0.0);
+    }
+}
+
+#elif defined(EXPOSURE)
+
+layout(local_size_x = 256) in;
+
+layout(std430, binding = 0) readonly buffer Partials {
+    vec4 partials[];
+};
+layout(std430, binding = 1) buffer State {
+    Exposure exposure;
+};
+layout(push_constant) uniform Params {
+    uint count;        // groups of the STATS pass
+    uint reset;        // no measurement last frame: take this one as it is
+    float min_samples; // fewer midtone samples (a dark or white frame): keep the last value
+    float rate;        // smoothing per frame
+};
+
+shared vec3 sums[256];
+
+void main() {
+    const uint index = gl_LocalInvocationIndex;
+    vec3 sum = vec3(0.0);
+    for (uint i = index; i < count; i += 256u) {
+        sum += partials[i].xyz;
+    }
+    sums[index] = sum;
+    barrier();
+    for (uint stride = 128u; stride > 0u; stride >>= 1u) {
+        if (index < stride) {
+            sums[index] += sums[index + stride];
+        }
+        barrier();
+    }
+    if (index != 0u) {
+        return;
+    }
+    const vec3 total = sums[0];
+    exposure.samples = total.z;
+    if (total.z >= min_samples && total.y > 0.0) {
+        const float measured = total.x / total.y;
+        exposure.log_scale = reset != 0u || exposure.valid == 0.0
+                                 ? measured
+                                 : mix(exposure.log_scale, measured, rate);
+        exposure.valid = 1.0;
+        exposure.frames += 1.0;
+    } else if (reset != 0u) {
+        // Back from menus or loading with too little to measure: no stale exposure.
+        exposure.valid = 0.0;
+    }
+}
+
+#elif defined(GAIN)
+
+layout(local_size_x = 8, local_size_y = 8) in;
+
+layout(binding = 0) uniform texture2D frame_tex;    // the finished frame (UI included)
+layout(binding = 1) uniform texture2D snapshot_tex; // the same before the first UI pass
+layout(binding = 2) uniform texture2D luma_tex;
+layout(std430, binding = 3) readonly buffer State {
+    Exposure exposure;
+};
+layout(binding = 4, r16f) uniform writeonly image2D gain_img;
+
+layout(push_constant) uniform Params {
+    float strength; // share of the scene's highlight range, in log2 (0: SDR, 1: all of it)
+    float white;    // peak / paper white: the most a pixel may reach (SDR white = 1)
+    float knee;     // linear brightest channel of the SDR frame where the gain starts
+    float unused;
+};
+
+// Unchanged up to halfway between SDR white and the peak, then towards the peak with the same
+// slope there: highlights above the display's range keep some gradation instead of clipping.
+float SoftCap(float x, float limit) {
+    const float start = 0.5 * (1.0 + limit);
+    if (x <= start) {
+        return x;
+    }
+    const float room = limit - start;
+    return start + room * (1.0 - exp(-(x - start) / room));
+}
+
+void main() {
+    const ivec2 pixel = ivec2(gl_GlobalInvocationID.xy);
+    const ivec2 size = imageSize(gain_img);
+    if (any(greaterThanEqual(pixel, size))) {
+        return;
+    }
+    const vec3 finished = texelFetch(frame_tex, pixel, 0).rgb;
+    const vec3 before_ui = texelFetch(snapshot_tex, pixel, 0).rgb;
+    // UI: whatever the UI passes changed (HUD, menus, subtitles, their translucent backgrounds).
+    const float ui = smoothstep(0.5 / 255.0, 2.5 / 255.0, Max3(abs(finished - before_ui)));
+    float gain = 1.0;
+    const float sdr = Max3(SdrToLinear(before_ui));
+    const float ramp = smoothstep(knee, 1.0, sdr);
+    if (exposure.valid > 0.0 && ramp > 0.0 && white > 1.0) {
+        // The brightest scene pixel of the 2x2 luma texels around (4x4 scene pixels): the
+        // highlight's footprint does not shimmer with the upscalers' sub-pixel jitter or the
+        // resampling between the scene and this frame (scaled presets).
+        const ivec2 luma_size = textureSize(luma_tex, 0);
+        const vec2 at = (vec2(pixel) + 0.5) * vec2(luma_size) / vec2(size) - 0.5;
+        const ivec2 base = ivec2(floor(at));
+        float peak = 0.0;
+        for (int y = 0; y < 2; ++y) {
+            for (int x = 0; x < 2; ++x) {
+                const ivec2 texel = clamp(base + ivec2(x, y), ivec2(0), luma_size - 1);
+                peak = max(peak, texelFetch(luma_tex, texel, 0).r);
+            }
+        }
+        // The scene's brightness on the SDR frame's scale, as the midtones are shown. Bloom and
+        // glare are in the SDR frame only: where it is brighter than the scene, gain stays 1.
+        const float scene = exp2(exposure.log_scale) * peak;
+        const float ratio = scene / max(sdr, 1e-4);
+        if (ratio > 1.0) {
+            const float target = SoftCap(sdr * exp2(strength * ramp * log2(ratio)), white);
+            gain = max(target / max(sdr, 1e-4), 1.0);
+        }
+    }
+    gain = mix(gain, 1.0, ui);
+    // Negative where UI was found (for the debug view); the encode uses the magnitude.
+    imageStore(gain_img, pixel, vec4(ui >= 0.5 ? -gain : gain, 0.0, 0.0, 0.0));
+}
+
+#endif
diff --git a/gpu/shadps4/video_core/host_shaders/hdr_output.frag b/gpu/shadps4/video_core/host_shaders/hdr_output.frag
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..b3d6246
--- /dev/null
+++ b/gpu/shadps4/video_core/host_shaders/hdr_output.frag
@@ -0,0 +1,159 @@
+// SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0-or-later
+// bbport: HDR output. The game's SDR picture (letterboxed into a swapchain-sized sRGB-encoded
+// image) and the settings menu's layer (premultiplied alpha, sRGB encoded) become the HDR
+// swapchain's signal: HDR10 (BT.2020 primaries, PQ) or scRGB (linear BT.709, 1.0 = 80 nits).
+// SDR white is shown at paper_white. Native mode (FLAG_NATIVE): the frame's gain map
+// (hdr_native.comp: how much brighter the game's own scene is where its tonemap compressed the
+// highlights; 1 for midtones and UI) brightens the highlights. Expand mode, and native frames
+// without a scene (menus, loading, movies): boost expands the SDR picture's highlights towards
+// the peak.
+// bbport: in native mode the game's UI (where the gain map is negative, and all of a frame
+// without a scene) has its own white (ui_white times paper white) and colours: ui_vivid of the
+// way from sRGB's (exact) to a wide-gamut SDR monitor's (the values taken as DCI-P3), which is
+// how most players have seen it - next to that, exact sRGB looks washed out.
+
+#version 450
+
+layout (location = 0) out vec4 color;
+
+layout (binding = 0) uniform sampler2D game;    // the letterboxed SDR frame, swapchain-sized
+layout (binding = 1) uniform sampler2D overlay; // the menu layer (FLAG_OVERLAY)
+layout (binding = 2) uniform sampler2D gain;    // the frame's gain map (FLAG_NATIVE), filtered
+
+layout (push_constant) uniform HdrSettings {
+    float paper_white; // nits of SDR white
+    float peak;        // nits the display reaches
+    float boost;       // 0: SDR white at paper white .. 1: SDR white at the peak
+    uint flags;
+    vec2 gain_offset;  // the frame's rectangle in the output: its top left corner, pixels,
+    vec2 gain_scale;   // and 1 / its size: output pixels -> gain map coordinates
+    float ui_white;    // native mode: the UI's white, times paper white
+    float ui_vivid;    // native mode: 0 the UI in sRGB's colours .. 1 in DCI-P3's
+} hdr;
+
+const uint FLAG_SCRGB = 1u;   // scRGB output, else HDR10
+const uint FLAG_OVERLAY = 2u; // the menu layer has something drawn
+const uint FLAG_NATIVE = 4u;  // the gain map belongs to this frame
+const uint FLAG_DEBUG = 8u;   // BB_HDR_DEBUG: the gain as a heat map
+const uint FLAG_UI_FRAME = 16u; // native mode, a frame without a scene: all of it is UI
+
+// Linear brightness (SDR white = 1) above which the highlights are expanded: high, so large
+// bright areas (fog, sky) stay near paper white and only lamps, fire and sparks reach the peak.
+const float KNEE = 0.8;
+
+// BT.709 -> BT.2020 primaries (columns).
+const mat3 BT709_TO_BT2020 = mat3(
+    0.6274, 0.0691, 0.0164,
+    0.3293, 0.9195, 0.0880,
+    0.0433, 0.0114, 0.8956);
+
+// DCI-P3 (D65) -> BT.709 primaries (columns): BT.709 values outside [0, 1] are the wider gamut,
+// which the BT.2020 and scRGB encodes below both carry.
+const mat3 P3_TO_BT709 = mat3(
+    1.2249, -0.0421, -0.0196,
+    -0.2249, 1.0421, -0.0786,
+    0.0, 0.0, 1.0983);
+
+// The game's picture is made for SDR displays, which show it with a 2.2 power curve. The
+// piecewise sRGB curve (what Windows uses for SDR content in HDR) lifts the shadows: dark
+// scenes turn grey and foggy. Bloodborne is mostly shadows, so 2.2 it is.
+vec3 SdrToLinear(vec3 c) {
+    return pow(max(c, vec3(0.0)), vec3(2.2));
+}
+
+// SMPTE ST 2084 inverse EOTF; y: luminance / 10000 nits.
+vec3 PqEncode(vec3 y) {
+    const float m1 = 0.1593017578125;
+    const float m2 = 78.84375;
+    const float c1 = 0.8359375;
+    const float c2 = 18.8515625;
+    const float c3 = 18.6875;
+    vec3 ym = pow(clamp(y, 0.0, 1.0), vec3(m1));
+    return pow((c1 + c2 * ym) / (1.0 + c3 * ym), vec3(m2));
+}
+
+// Inverse tone map of the brightest channel (hue and saturation kept): unchanged up to KNEE,
+// then a curve with the same slope there that reaches `white` at SDR white.
+vec3 ExpandHighlights(vec3 rgb, float white) {
+    float m = max(rgb.r, max(rgb.g, rgb.b));
+    if (white <= 1.0 || m <= KNEE) {
+        return rgb;
+    }
+    float t = (m - KNEE) / (1.0 - KNEE);
+    float expanded = m + (white - 1.0) * t * t;
+    return rgb * (expanded / m);
+}
+
+// Debug view colours: blue (a little gain) .. cyan .. green .. yellow .. red (the peak).
+vec3 Heat(float t) {
+    t = clamp(t, 0.0, 1.0);
+    return clamp(vec3(1.5 - abs(4.0 * t - 3.0), 1.5 - abs(4.0 * t - 2.0),
+                      1.5 - abs(4.0 * t - 1.0)), 0.0, 1.0);
+}
+
+void main() {
+    ivec2 pixel = ivec2(gl_FragCoord.xy);
+    vec3 game_srgb = texelFetch(game, pixel, 0).rgb;
+    float peak_white = max(hdr.peak / hdr.paper_white, 1.0);
+    float white = max(mix(1.0, peak_white, clamp(hdr.boost, 0.0, 1.0)), 1.0);
+    vec3 sdr_linear = SdrToLinear(game_srgb);
+    vec3 linear;
+    float applied = 1.0; // the brightest channel's gain (debug view)
+    bool ui = false;
+    float ui_amount = (hdr.flags & FLAG_UI_FRAME) != 0u ? 1.0 : 0.0; // filtered: soft UI edges
+    if ((hdr.flags & FLAG_NATIVE) != 0u) {
+        // Outside the frame (letterbox bars) the map is not sampled: gain 1.
+        vec2 uv = (gl_FragCoord.xy - hdr.gain_offset) * hdr.gain_scale;
+        if (all(greaterThanEqual(uv, vec2(0.0))) && all(lessThanEqual(uv, vec2(1.0)))) {
+            float g = textureLod(gain, uv, 0.0).r;
+            ui = g < 0.0;
+            ui_amount = clamp(-g, 0.0, 1.0);
+            applied = max(abs(g), 1.0);
+        }
+        // The map keeps the brightest channel within the peak; filtering and the letterbox
+        // blit can step over it by a little.
+        linear = min(sdr_linear * applied, vec3(peak_white));
+    } else {
+        linear = ExpandHighlights(sdr_linear, white);
+        float m = max(sdr_linear.r, max(sdr_linear.g, sdr_linear.b));
+        applied = m > 0.0 ? max(linear.r, max(linear.g, linear.b)) / m : 1.0;
+    }
+    if (ui_amount > 0.0) {
+        linear *= mix(1.0, hdr.ui_white, ui_amount);
+        linear = mix(linear, P3_TO_BT709 * linear, ui_amount * clamp(hdr.ui_vivid, 0.0, 1.0));
+        linear = min(linear, vec3(peak_white));
+    }
+    if ((hdr.flags & FLAG_DEBUG) != 0u) {
+        // The picture in grey at a third of paper white, the gain over it from blue (a little)
+        // to red (the peak), UI that native mode keeps at paper white in magenta. Top left
+        // corner: green while native mode has this frame's gain map, orange while it falls back
+        // to expand (or in expand mode).
+        float y = dot(sdr_linear, vec3(0.2126, 0.7152, 0.0722));
+        vec3 shown = vec3(y * 0.33);
+        if (applied > 1.001) {
+            shown = mix(shown, Heat(log2(applied) / max(log2(peak_white), 0.1)), 0.8);
+        }
+        if (ui) {
+            shown = mix(shown, vec3(1.0, 0.0, 1.0), 0.5);
+        }
+        if (gl_FragCoord.x < 48.0 && gl_FragCoord.y < 48.0) {
+            shown = (hdr.flags & FLAG_NATIVE) != 0u ? vec3(0.0, 1.0, 0.0) : vec3(1.0, 0.5, 0.0);
+        }
+        linear = shown;
+    }
+    if ((hdr.flags & FLAG_OVERLAY) != 0u) {
+        // The menu over the frame as the SDR path draws it (blended in sRGB), at paper white:
+        // the menu is not expanded.
+        vec4 menu = texelFetch(overlay, pixel, 0);
+        if (menu.a > 0.0) {
+            vec3 sdr = SdrToLinear(clamp(menu.rgb + game_srgb * (1.0 - menu.a), 0.0, 1.0));
+            linear = mix(linear, sdr, clamp(menu.a * 4.0, 0.0, 1.0));
+        }
+    }
+    if ((hdr.flags & FLAG_SCRGB) != 0u) {
+        color = vec4(linear * (hdr.paper_white / 80.0), 1.0);
+    } else {
+        vec3 nits = max(BT709_TO_BT2020 * linear, vec3(0.0)) * hdr.paper_white;
+        color = vec4(PqEncode(nits / 10000.0), 1.0);
+    }
+}
diff --git a/gpu/shadps4/video_core/renderer_vulkan/host_passes/hdr_pass.cpp b/gpu/shadps4/video_core/renderer_vulkan/host_passes/hdr_pass.cpp
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..a263cce
--- /dev/null
+++ b/gpu/shadps4/video_core/renderer_vulkan/host_passes/hdr_pass.cpp
@@ -0,0 +1,257 @@
+// SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0-or-later
+// bbport: HDR output encode pass (hdr_pass.h), modelled on the post-processing pass.
+
+#include "video_core/renderer_vulkan/host_passes/hdr_pass.h"
+
+#include "common/assert.h"
+#include "core/emulator_settings.h"
+#include "video_core/host_shaders/fs_tri_vert.h"
+#include "video_core/host_shaders/hdr_output_frag.h"
+#include "video_core/renderer_vulkan/vk_platform.h"
+#include "video_core/renderer_vulkan/vk_shader_util.h"
+
+#include <array>
+
+namespace Vulkan::HostPasses {
+
+void HdrOutputPass::Create(vk::Device device, vk::Format output_format) {
+    const std::array bindings{
+        vk::DescriptorSetLayoutBinding{
+            .binding = 0,
+            .descriptorType = vk::DescriptorType::eCombinedImageSampler,
+            .descriptorCount = 1,
+            .stageFlags = vk::ShaderStageFlagBits::eFragment,
+        },
+        vk::DescriptorSetLayoutBinding{
+            .binding = 1,
+            .descriptorType = vk::DescriptorType::eCombinedImageSampler,
+            .descriptorCount = 1,
+            .stageFlags = vk::ShaderStageFlagBits::eFragment,
+        },
+        vk::DescriptorSetLayoutBinding{
+            .binding = 2,
+            .descriptorType = vk::DescriptorType::eCombinedImageSampler,
+            .descriptorCount = 1,
+            .stageFlags = vk::ShaderStageFlagBits::eFragment,
+        },
+    };
+    desc_set_layout = Check<"create HDR output descriptor set layout">(
+        device.createDescriptorSetLayoutUnique(vk::DescriptorSetLayoutCreateInfo{
+            .flags = vk::DescriptorSetLayoutCreateFlagBits::ePushDescriptorKHR,
+            .bindingCount = static_cast<u32>(bindings.size()),
+            .pBindings = bindings.data(),
+        }));
+
+    const vk::PushConstantRange push_constants{
+        .stageFlags = vk::ShaderStageFlagBits::eFragment,
+        .offset = 0,
+        .size = sizeof(Settings),
+    };
+    pipeline_layout = Check<"create HDR output pipeline layout">(
+        device.createPipelineLayoutUnique(vk::PipelineLayoutCreateInfo{
+            .setLayoutCount = 1U,
+            .pSetLayouts = &*desc_set_layout,
+            .pushConstantRangeCount = 1,
+            .pPushConstantRanges = &push_constants,
+        }));
+
+    const auto vs_module = CompileSPV(FS_TRI_VERT, device);
+    ASSERT(vs_module);
+    SetObjectName(device, vs_module, "fs_tri.vert");
+    const auto fs_module = CompileSPV(HDR_OUTPUT_FRAG, device);
+    ASSERT(fs_module);
+    SetObjectName(device, fs_module, "hdr_output.frag");
+
+    const std::array shaders_ci{
+        vk::PipelineShaderStageCreateInfo{
+            .stage = vk::ShaderStageFlagBits::eVertex,
+            .module = vs_module,
+            .pName = "main",
+        },
+        vk::PipelineShaderStageCreateInfo{
+            .stage = vk::ShaderStageFlagBits::eFragment,
+            .module = fs_module,
+            .pName = "main",
+        },
+    };
+
+    const vk::PipelineRenderingCreateInfo pipeline_rendering_ci{
+        .colorAttachmentCount = 1,
+        .pColorAttachmentFormats = &output_format,
+    };
+    const vk::PipelineVertexInputStateCreateInfo vertex_input_info{};
+    const vk::PipelineInputAssemblyStateCreateInfo input_assembly{
+        .topology = vk::PrimitiveTopology::eTriangleList,
+    };
+    const vk::Viewport viewport{
+        .width = 1.0f,
+        .height = 1.0f,
+        .minDepth = 0.0f,
+        .maxDepth = 1.0f,
+    };
+    const vk::Rect2D scissor{.extent = {1, 1}};
+    const vk::PipelineViewportStateCreateInfo viewport_info{
+        .viewportCount = 1,
+        .pViewports = &viewport,
+        .scissorCount = 1,
+        .pScissors = &scissor,
+    };
+    const vk::PipelineRasterizationStateCreateInfo raster_state{
+        .depthClampEnable = false,
+        .rasterizerDiscardEnable = false,
+        .polygonMode = vk::PolygonMode::eFill,
+        .cullMode = vk::CullModeFlagBits::eNone,
+        .frontFace = vk::FrontFace::eClockwise,
+        .depthBiasEnable = false,
+        .lineWidth = 1.0f,
+    };
+    const vk::PipelineMultisampleStateCreateInfo multisampling{
+        .rasterizationSamples = vk::SampleCountFlagBits::e1,
+    };
+    const vk::PipelineColorBlendAttachmentState attachment{
+        .blendEnable = false,
+        .colorWriteMask = vk::ColorComponentFlagBits::eR | vk::ColorComponentFlagBits::eG |
+                          vk::ColorComponentFlagBits::eB | vk::ColorComponentFlagBits::eA,
+    };
+    const vk::PipelineColorBlendStateCreateInfo color_blending{
+        .logicOpEnable = false,
+        .logicOp = vk::LogicOp::eCopy,
+        .attachmentCount = 1,
+        .pAttachments = &attachment,
+    };
+    const std::array dynamic_states{
+        vk::DynamicState::eViewport,
+        vk::DynamicState::eScissor,
+    };
+    const vk::PipelineDynamicStateCreateInfo dynamic_info{
+        .dynamicStateCount = static_cast<u32>(dynamic_states.size()),
+        .pDynamicStates = dynamic_states.data(),
+    };
+    const vk::GraphicsPipelineCreateInfo pipeline_info{
+        .pNext = &pipeline_rendering_ci,
+        .stageCount = static_cast<u32>(shaders_ci.size()),
+        .pStages = shaders_ci.data(),
+        .pVertexInputState = &vertex_input_info,
+        .pInputAssemblyState = &input_assembly,
+        .pViewportState = &viewport_info,
+        .pRasterizationState = &raster_state,
+        .pMultisampleState = &multisampling,
+        .pColorBlendState = &color_blending,
+        .pDynamicState = &dynamic_info,
+        .layout = *pipeline_layout,
+    };
+    pipeline = Check<"create HDR output pipeline">(
+        device.createGraphicsPipelineUnique(/*pipeline_cache*/ {}, pipeline_info));
+
+    device.destroyShaderModule(vs_module);
+    device.destroyShaderModule(fs_module);
+
+    // The shader reads texels (texelFetch); a combined image sampler still needs a sampler.
+    sampler = Check<"create HDR output sampler">(device.createSamplerUnique(vk::SamplerCreateInfo{
+        .magFilter = vk::Filter::eNearest,
+        .minFilter = vk::Filter::eNearest,
+        .mipmapMode = vk::SamplerMipmapMode::eNearest,
+        .addressModeU = vk::SamplerAddressMode::eClampToEdge,
+        .addressModeV = vk::SamplerAddressMode::eClampToEdge,
+        .addressModeW = vk::SamplerAddressMode::eClampToEdge,
+    }));
+    // bbport: native mode's gain map has the display buffer's size, not the output's: filtered
+    // like the letterbox blit filters the frame.
+    gain_sampler = Check<"create HDR gain sampler">(device.createSamplerUnique(vk::SamplerCreateInfo{
+        .magFilter = vk::Filter::eLinear,
+        .minFilter = vk::Filter::eLinear,
+        .mipmapMode = vk::SamplerMipmapMode::eNearest,
+        .addressModeU = vk::SamplerAddressMode::eClampToEdge,
+        .addressModeV = vk::SamplerAddressMode::eClampToEdge,
+        .addressModeW = vk::SamplerAddressMode::eClampToEdge,
+    }));
+}
+
+void HdrOutputPass::Render(vk::CommandBuffer cmdbuf, vk::ImageView game, vk::ImageView overlay,
+                           vk::ImageView gain, vk::ImageLayout gain_layout, vk::ImageView output,
+                           vk::Extent2D extent, const Settings& settings) {
+    if (EmulatorSettings.IsVkHostMarkersEnabled()) {
+        cmdbuf.beginDebugUtilsLabelEXT(vk::DebugUtilsLabelEXT{
+            .pLabelName = "Host/HDR output",
+        });
+    }
+
+    const std::array image_infos{
+        vk::DescriptorImageInfo{
+            .sampler = *sampler,
+            .imageView = game,
+            .imageLayout = vk::ImageLayout::eShaderReadOnlyOptimal,
+        },
+        vk::DescriptorImageInfo{
+            .sampler = *sampler,
+            .imageView = overlay,
+            .imageLayout = vk::ImageLayout::eShaderReadOnlyOptimal,
+        },
+        vk::DescriptorImageInfo{
+            .sampler = *gain_sampler,
+            .imageView = gain,
+            .imageLayout = gain_layout,
+        },
+    };
+    const std::array set_writes{
+        vk::WriteDescriptorSet{
+            .dstSet = VK_NULL_HANDLE,
+            .dstBinding = 0,
+            .dstArrayElement = 0,
+            .descriptorCount = 1,
+            .descriptorType = vk::DescriptorType::eCombinedImageSampler,
+            .pImageInfo = &image_infos[0],
+        },
+        vk::WriteDescriptorSet{
+            .dstSet = VK_NULL_HANDLE,
+            .dstBinding = 1,
+            .dstArrayElement = 0,
+            .descriptorCount = 1,
+            .descriptorType = vk::DescriptorType::eCombinedImageSampler,
+            .pImageInfo = &image_infos[1],
+        },
+        vk::WriteDescriptorSet{
+            .dstSet = VK_NULL_HANDLE,
+            .dstBinding = 2,
+            .dstArrayElement = 0,
+            .descriptorCount = 1,
+            .descriptorType = vk::DescriptorType::eCombinedImageSampler,
+            .pImageInfo = &image_infos[2],
+        },
+    };
+
+    const vk::RenderingAttachmentInfo attachment{
+        .imageView = output,
+        .imageLayout = vk::ImageLayout::eColorAttachmentOptimal,
+        // Every pixel is written by the full-screen triangle.
+        .loadOp = vk::AttachmentLoadOp::eDontCare,
+        .storeOp = vk::AttachmentStoreOp::eStore,
+    };
+    const vk::RenderingInfo rendering_info{
+        .renderArea = {.extent = extent},
+        .layerCount = 1,
+        .colorAttachmentCount = 1,
+        .pColorAttachments = &attachment,
+    };
+
+    cmdbuf.bindPipeline(vk::PipelineBindPoint::eGraphics, *pipeline);
+    cmdbuf.setViewport(0, vk::Viewport{
+                              .width = static_cast<float>(extent.width),
+                              .height = static_cast<float>(extent.height),
+                              .minDepth = 0.0f,
+                              .maxDepth = 1.0f,
+                          });
+    cmdbuf.setScissor(0, vk::Rect2D{.extent = extent});
+    cmdbuf.pushDescriptorSetKHR(vk::PipelineBindPoint::eGraphics, *pipeline_layout, 0, set_writes);
+    cmdbuf.pushConstants(*pipeline_layout, vk::ShaderStageFlagBits::eFragment, 0,
+                         sizeof(Settings), &settings);
+    cmdbuf.beginRendering(rendering_info);
+    cmdbuf.draw(3, 1, 0, 0);
+    cmdbuf.endRendering();
+
+    if (EmulatorSettings.IsVkHostMarkersEnabled()) {
+        cmdbuf.endDebugUtilsLabelEXT();
+    }
+}
+
+} // namespace Vulkan::HostPasses
diff --git a/gpu/shadps4/video_core/renderer_vulkan/host_passes/hdr_pass.h b/gpu/shadps4/video_core/renderer_vulkan/host_passes/hdr_pass.h
new file mode 100644
